WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.580
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 44.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.020
It's May 20th, 2026.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.800
Some awesome AI developments today,

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.840
and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.060
Let's get into it.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.060
So imagine you have a super organized friend who never sleeps

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:18.580
and always knows exactly what you need next.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.540
That is basically what Google showed off this week

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.640
at their big yearly event called IO.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.020
The main new tool is called Gemini Spark,

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.760
and always on AI agent that watches topics you care about

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.940
and jumps in with help when things change.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.120
Instead of waiting for you to ask one question at a time

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.340
like a regular chatbot,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.300
this agent can plan events, send reminders,

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.840
or pull together a daily briefing on its own.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.460
Think of it like having a personal assistant

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.140
who quietly checks your calendar,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.140
notices when a research topic gets new updates

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.160
and pops up with the next step without being asked.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.660
This matters because most of us already use AI

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:58.660
for quick homework tips or fun ideas,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.240
but now the same tech is learning to handle longer jobs

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.560
that take many steps.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.760
A student might ask it to track a school project

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.880
and get notified when fresh information appears online.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.320
Someone into gaming or creative hobbies could use it

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.300
to keep a running list of ideas or clips

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.520
without typing every single detail themselves.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.600
The personal side is that these agents need more access

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.280
to your data to work well.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.920
So Google is asking people to trust it

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.940
with more private information than before.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.940
That raises fair questions about privacy,

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.120
but it also opens the door to AI

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.980
that feels less like a search box

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.700
and more like a helpful teammate who has your back.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.520
Right now you can try early versions

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.140
through Google's testing programs

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.800
by heading to the Google app or their Gemini site

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.820
and starting a conversation about planning something simple

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.220
like a study session.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.560
Watch how it suggests next steps

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.520
instead of just answering one question.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.780
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.220
where we look under the hood.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.180
You know how when you are playing a video game,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.740
your character can walk around, open doors and complete quests

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.200
even though it does not actually think like a person.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.800
It just follows a set of rules that tell it what to do next

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.860
based on what is happening in the game world.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.080
Now picture that same idea,

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.740
but instead of a game character,

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.660
it is an AI helper that breaks a big request

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:20.980
into smaller steps.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.160
The AI decides which tool or website to use for each step

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.660
and keeps going until the whole job is finished.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.040
It does not just guess the next word like a chatbot.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.560
It actually checks results and adjusts

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:34.720
if something does not work,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.700
almost like a chef who tastes the soup

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.720
and adds more salt if it needs it.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.000
And that is basically what an AI agent is doing

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.060
when it plans your day or research is a topic for you.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:47.820
It runs a loop where it looks at the goal,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.020
picks an action, sees what happened,

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.760
then picks the next action.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.840
The more loops it can run without getting stuck,

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.680
the more it feels like it is truly taking care of things

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:58.440
on its own.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.120
Think of the loop like following a checklist on a road trip

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.860
or you keep checking the map and adjusting your route

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.460
until you reach the destination.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.040
So next time someone says,

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.320
agentic AI, you can tell them it is basically a video game character

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.500
that can run errands in the real world

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.480
instead of just inside a game.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:19.220
Not so scary, right?

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.900
Now let's look at some cool stuff you can try yourself today.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.240
Google's AI studio now lets anyone describe an app

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.800
in plain words and get a working Android app

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.020
generated automatically.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.020
It turns your idea into actual code

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.760
and gives you a preview you can test right on your phone.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.280
This is perfect if you have ever wanted

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.520
to make a simple game, study tool, or social app

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.040
but did not know where to start.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.940
Head to Google's AI studio website,

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.960
sign in with a Google account and type something

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.340
like a flash card app for Spanish vocabulary

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:53.800
with a cute mascot.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.480
Hit generate and play with the result right in your browser.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.860
Think of it like describing a dream house to an architect

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.220
who instantly hands you the finished blueprints

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.120
you can walk through.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.340
Next up is a tool called Agora 1

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.580
that can take the old, gold and eye game

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.980
and turn it into an AI powered simulation

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.920
where up to four players move around at the same time.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.820
Two different AI models work together

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.320
with one handling what happens in the game

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.480
and the other drawing the pictures you see.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.080
It is a peek at how AI might create

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.120
entire playable worlds for games

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.320
or even robot training in the future.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.940
Search online for Agora 1, gold and eye

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.380
to watch the demo videos

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.040
and imagine what it would feel like

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.800
to explore a fully AI generated level.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.380
Picture it like breathing life into an old photograph

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.480
so the people inside can start moving

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:45.900
and reacting on their own.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.280
Here are a couple of quick bits to wrap up the news.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:51.340
At a recent college graduation,

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:54.820
former Google CEO, Eric Schmidt got booed

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.700
when he talked about how fast AI is changing everything.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.460
It shows that not everyone is excited

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.180
about AI moving this quickly

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.000
and students especially are asking hard questions

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.320
about jobs in the future.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:08.060
On a brighter note,

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.620
a simple video explains how regular people

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.400
with no coding required can fine tune an AI model

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.500
so it better matches their own needs.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.060
The tools are getting easier every month

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.700
whether you want help with specific school subjects

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:23.160
or creative projects.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.380
Think of fine tuning like adding your own favorite

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:28.080
spices to a basic recipe

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:31.420
so the final dish tastes exactly the way you like it.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:32.820
That's it for today.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.680
Remember every AI expert started exactly

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.040
where you are right now.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:39.740
If something we talked about today made you curious,

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:40.780
go try it.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.540
That's literally how learning works.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.960
Stay curious, keep experimenting

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:46.340
and we'll see you tomorrow.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.620
And if you'd rather watch than listen,

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.540
find us on YouTube at at Nerra Network.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:52.940
Links in the show notes.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.920
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.100
Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
